turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (3) turnkey; urgency=low

  * bin/keel-archive-check refuses a trusted=yes on the project archive rather
    than on every apt source in the build tree. The captured pool of decision
    0012 sets Trusted: yes on purpose, for a file: index generated on this
    machine from files keel-pool verify checks against the same digests apt
    does, so the wider rule would have failed every pinned build. What the pool
    does is the pool's business; the project archive is verified (tracker#7).

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 20:10:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (2) turnkey; urgency=low

  * The build verifies the project's own APT archive instead of reading it
    unverified. The staging distribution has been signed since 2026-09-27, but
    the build environment had no copy of the public key and the source entry
    said [trusted=yes], which switches verification off: apt printed
    "W: OpenPGP signature verification failed ... Missing key
    8CFD1A4841448B2227341CEB202CACBD0E97090A" and installed inithooks,
    confconsole and keel anyway. The public half of the staging key is now
    installed into the build tree as
    /etc/apt/keyrings/keel-staging-keyring.asc, the source entry names it
    through signed-by, and apt-get update runs with --error-on=any, so a
    signature that cannot be checked fails the build instead of warning
    (tracker#7).

  * bin/keel-archive-check does the same checks itself rather than trusting
    apt to have complained: the copied package index is the live one, the
    source entry names the keyring and this distribution, no apt source in
    the tree says trusted=yes, and the signature on the copied InRelease
    verifies against the named key with gpgv. It runs where the copy is made
    and again on the tree that is about to be configured, which is a step
    where no apt-get update runs at all.

  * conf.d/main removes the keyring with the source entry and the copy of the
    archive: the staging key signs whatever the build host produced, so an
    image that kept it would carry trust in a nightly. The removelist
    common/removelists-final/turnkey takes all three out as well, whatever a
    recipe does.

  * The floor for keel rises from 0.2.1 to 0.3.5. The two node gate configures
    replication from the instance description on the booted machine, which
    needs the database section, the role configuration apply writes and
    promotion; a layer carrying keel 0.2.1 answers "database: unknown top
    level key" and the gate fails, as it did on keel-mariadb#10.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sun, 27 Sep 2026 19:30:00 +0000

turnkey-mariadb-19.0 (1) turnkey; urgency=low

  * Initial release of the MariaDB database layer for Keel, compatible with
    TurnKey Linux appliances. Built as a layer on core:
    bt-layer mariadb --parent core.

  * MariaDB 11.8 from Debian 13 (Trixie), listening on ::1 and 127.0.0.1
    only (overlay/etc/mysql/mariadb.conf.d/99-keel-bind.cnf).

  * webmin-mysql, so the database is administered from the Webmin panel
    core already carries, at https://[address]:12321.

  * An administrative account, 'admin', created unusable at build time and
    given its password at first boot from the instance description:
    secrets.db_password renders to DB_PASS, which
    firstboot.d/35mysqlpass reads. This layer has no Adminer, so it ships
    the hook the Adminer overlay of common would otherwise have brought.

  * Adminer, lighttpd and php-fpm, which the upstream mysql appliance
    bundles, are deliberately left out: they belong to the web stack that
    LAMP builds on this layer.

 -- Keel Linux maintainers <admin@keellinux.org>  Sat, 27 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000
